  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
  <details class="faq-item">
    <summary><strong>Why is there still out-of-pocket cash after using miles for flights?</strong></summary>
    <p>Frequent flyer miles only cover the airline’s base fare. Government taxes, airport security charges, international departure fees, and carrier-imposed fuel surcharges (YQ/YR) cannot be covered by miles and must be paid in cash.</p>
  </details>
  <details class="faq-item">
    <summary><strong>Do hotel award stays charge resort fees?</strong></summary>
    <p>Policies vary by loyalty program: World of Hyatt and Hilton Honors waive resort/destination fees on 100% point award bookings. Marriott Bonvoy and IHG One Rewards typically require guests to pay resort fees in cash at checkout even on award stays.</p>
  </details>
  <details class="faq-item">
    <summary><strong>When is it better to pay cash instead of redeeming points?</strong></summary>
    <p>You should pay cash if: 1. Award taxes and fees are excessively high, creating minimal or negative net savings; 2. The realized CPP is below conservative benchmark valuations (e.g. under 1.2¢ for airline miles or under 0.6¢ for hotel points); 3. Cash fares are on steep discount and earning cash/flight credits provides superior long-term utility.</p>
  </details>
  <details class="faq-item">
    <summary><strong>Is my travel budget data sent to any server?</strong></summary>
    <p>No. All calculations are executed 100% locally in your web browser. We do not store, track, or transmit your financial data or travel plans to any external server.</p>
  </details>

  <h2>Disclaimer & Editorial Notes</h2>
  <p class="disclaimer">
    Calculations and recommendations are provided for trip planning purposes and do not constitute financial advice. Cash prices, award space availability, loyalty program transfer rules, and mandatory taxes are subject to continuous change by airlines and hotel chains.<br>
    Last verified: 2026-08-24 · Points & Miles Calculator Research Team
  </p>
